WEBVTT

00:00:00.597 --> 00:00:01.550
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:04.510
Today I am not going to teach you how to hold a brush.

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:09.950
I want to sit down and talk with you about the things that usually take a few years in the trade to understand:

00:00:10.238 --> 00:00:12.366
why, with the same bottle of gel,

00:00:12.316 --> 00:00:15.642
one technician gives the client three weeks of wear,

00:00:15.592 --> 00:00:18.350
and another sees it lift within three days.

00:00:18.928 --> 00:00:21.068
Let me tell you about something very common.

00:00:21.403 --> 00:00:25.388
A new technician, with beautiful brushwork and very crisp lines.

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:30.348
But clients keep coming back after a few days, because the gel lifts at the cuticle.

00:00:30.691 --> 00:00:34.668
She changes gel brands, changes lamps, and it still lifts.

00:00:35.002 --> 00:00:37.628
In the end, the problem was not in the bottle.

00:00:37.923 --> 00:00:40.348
It was that she did not yet understand the nail.

00:00:40.952 --> 00:00:44.027
To understand, you need to know what the nail is made of.

00:00:44.462 --> 00:00:47.147
The hard part we paint on is called the nail plate.

00:00:47.561 --> 00:00:50.267
It is made of keratin, in many thin layers.

00:00:50.663 --> 00:00:54.587
The nail grows from the matrix, hidden under the skin at the base of the nail.

00:00:55.016 --> 00:00:58.282
If a technician files or presses too hard in this area,

00:00:58.232 --> 00:01:01.147
the nail can grow out ridged, sometimes for good.

00:01:01.737 --> 00:01:04.346
Above the base of the nail is living skin.

00:01:04.822 --> 00:01:08.746
Its job is to seal, so bacteria cannot reach the matrix.

00:01:09.062 --> 00:01:10.507
We do not cut this part.

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:15.090
The thin, clear layer of skin stuck to the nail plate is the dead skin,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.147
and that is what we need to remove.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.405
Now the most important point.

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:21.645
Gel does not soak into the nail.

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:23.325
It only sticks to the surface.

00:01:23.663 --> 00:01:25.884
So the surface must meet three conditions:

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:29.165
free of dead skin, free of oil, and dry.

00:01:29.819 --> 00:01:34.273
If a thin layer of skin is left, the gel sticks to the skin, not to the nail,

00:01:34.223 --> 00:01:36.045
and within a few days it lifts.

00:01:36.532 --> 00:01:39.885
If there is oil from the skin of the hands, the gel slides off.

00:01:40.541 --> 00:01:41.258
And water?

00:01:41.644 --> 00:01:44.458
The nail plate absorbs water, like a sponge.

00:01:44.695 --> 00:01:47.898
It swells, and when it dries, it shrinks back.

00:01:48.236 --> 00:01:49.658
The gel cannot shrink with it.

00:01:50.162 --> 00:01:54.226
That is why many good technicians choose to work dry, with no soaking,

00:01:54.176 --> 00:01:55.258
before gel polish.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.658
And buffing is only to take the shine off.

00:01:58.045 --> 00:02:00.378
Buffing hard does not make the gel stick better;

00:02:00.624 --> 00:02:03.182
it only makes the nail thinner, and next time,

00:02:03.132 --> 00:02:05.418
the client will feel burning in the lamp.

00:02:06.035 --> 00:02:07.345
Now, the lamp.

00:02:07.672 --> 00:02:10.705
Gel contains a substance called a photoinitiator.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.203
When it meets the right kind of light,

00:02:13.153 --> 00:02:16.465
it starts a reaction that turns the gel from liquid to solid.

00:02:16.942 --> 00:02:20.945
Each brand makes its gel for a particular lamp, and a particular time.

00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:25.363
With a lamp from another brand, or an old lamp that has grown weak,

00:02:25.313 --> 00:02:27.505
the surface of the gel may feel hard,

00:02:27.691 --> 00:02:30.145
but underneath it is still not fully cured.

00:02:30.322 --> 00:02:33.265
Under cured gel is one of the main causes of allergy.

00:02:33.809 --> 00:02:35.999
That is also why you paint thin coats.

00:02:36.407 --> 00:02:40.639
Light cannot get all the way through a thick layer of gel, especially a dark color.

00:02:41.101 --> 00:02:44.103
In a thick coat, the top cures first, shrinks,

00:02:44.053 --> 00:02:47.119
and wrinkles the still liquid layer underneath.

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:50.079
When gel cures, it also gives off heat.

00:02:50.502 --> 00:02:54.239
If a client says it is burning in the lamp, do not tell her to put up with it.

00:02:54.498 --> 00:02:57.759
Let her take the hand out for a few seconds, then put it back in.

00:02:58.171 --> 00:02:59.919
And next time, paint thinner.

00:03:00.568 --> 00:03:04.249
And the sticky layer on the surface after curing is not faulty gel.

00:03:04.798 --> 00:03:07.683
It is a very thin layer of gel that cannot cure,

00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:09.929
because it is in contact with the air.

00:03:10.386 --> 00:03:13.518
It is uncured gel too, so do not let it touch the skin,

00:03:13.468 --> 00:03:15.929
whether the skin of the client or your own.

00:03:16.538 --> 00:03:20.668
I want to talk about allergy in detail, because it concerns your own career.

00:03:21.166 --> 00:03:23.388
A gel allergy does not come immediately.

00:03:23.722 --> 00:03:27.548
It comes after many months, sometimes years, of contact.

00:03:28.046 --> 00:03:32.753
At first, there is itching around the nails, red skin, small blisters,

00:03:32.703 --> 00:03:35.948
and sometimes the nail lifting off the nail bed.

00:03:36.297 --> 00:03:38.988
And once you are allergic, it is usually for life.

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:43.388
Some technicians have had to leave the trade, because they can no longer touch gel.

00:03:43.577 --> 00:03:47.552
Dermatologists also warn that this allergy can cause problems later on,

00:03:47.502 --> 00:03:49.708
with dental work or joint replacements,

00:03:49.894 --> 00:03:52.908
because those materials belong to the same family as gel.

00:03:53.513 --> 00:03:54.597
So the rule is:

00:03:54.707 --> 00:03:57.077
uncured gel never touches the skin.

00:03:57.545 --> 00:04:00.837
Wear nitrile gloves, and change them when they get gel on them.

00:04:01.323 --> 00:04:03.797
Wipe the brush handle and the bottle neck clean.

00:04:04.306 --> 00:04:06.677
Never wipe excess gel off with your finger.

00:04:07.106 --> 00:04:10.517
If gel gets on the skin of the client, wipe it off before curing.

00:04:10.867 --> 00:04:12.677
And always cure for the full time.

00:04:13.335 --> 00:04:14.457
On regulations.

00:04:14.705 --> 00:04:18.141
In the European Union, since 1 September 2025,

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:22.777
the photoinitiator called TPO has been banned in nail products.

00:04:23.125 --> 00:04:26.377
Salons may not keep using it, not even stock bought before.

00:04:26.714 --> 00:04:29.577
Check the ingredients on every bottle of gel you have.

00:04:29.864 --> 00:04:34.137
Buy gel from suppliers within Europe, with a clear label and ingredient list.

00:04:34.262 --> 00:04:38.822
With cheap products of unknown origin, the people who carry the risk are you,

00:04:38.772 --> 00:04:39.817
and your clients.

00:04:40.399 --> 00:04:42.620
Now, experience with clients.

00:04:43.084 --> 00:04:43.740
First:

00:04:43.862 --> 00:04:46.060
always look and ask before you start.

00:04:46.407 --> 00:04:51.638
If a nail is discolored, thickened or crumbly, or the skin is red and swollen,

00:04:51.588 --> 00:04:53.100
do not do the service,

00:04:53.287 --> 00:04:55.180
and advise the client to see a doctor.

00:04:55.518 --> 00:04:59.340
Turning one client away today protects the reputation of the whole salon.

00:04:59.755 --> 00:05:00.380
Second:

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.180
be honest with clients about how long it lasts.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.580
Gel looks good for two to three weeks.

00:05:05.841 --> 00:05:09.707
Leave it longer, and the nail grows out, the stress point shifts,

00:05:09.657 --> 00:05:11.820
and it breaks and lifts more easily.

00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:13.104
Third:

00:05:13.274 --> 00:05:15.344
tell the client not to pick off the gel.

00:05:15.691 --> 00:05:18.784
Picking gel peels off layers of the natural nail with it.

00:05:19.133 --> 00:05:22.340
The nail gets thinner, next time the gel holds even less well,

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.784
and then the client thinks your skills are poor.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:26.227
Fourth:

00:05:26.391 --> 00:05:30.307
when a client comes back because of lifting, do not rush to blame her.

00:05:30.676 --> 00:05:32.067
Look at where the gel lifted.

00:05:32.474 --> 00:05:35.667
Lifting at the cuticle usually means skin was left behind.

00:05:35.952 --> 00:05:39.628
Lifting at the tip usually means the free edge was not capped,

00:05:39.578 --> 00:05:42.787
or the client uses her nails to pry or to open things.

00:05:43.135 --> 00:05:47.827
Lifting in whole patches usually means oil, moisture, or under cured gel.

00:05:48.066 --> 00:05:51.587
Each kind of lifting is a reminder, so you can fix the right thing.

00:05:52.241 --> 00:05:52.794
You see.

00:05:53.077 --> 00:05:55.514
Beautiful gel polish comes from your hands.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.994
But gel polish that lasts, and is safe, comes from understanding.

00:06:00.583 --> 00:06:04.880
A technician who understands why each step is done will do good work anywhere,

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:05.914
with any gel brand.

00:06:06.264 --> 00:06:08.874
And now, take the short quiz below the video.

00:06:09.131 --> 00:06:10.234
See you in the next lesson.
